WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.440
Many differential equations cannot be solved by formulas.

2
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:09.240
For example, y prime equals x squared plus y squared.

3
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:15.072
In this lesson we learn to find the solution approximately, as a table of numbers.

4
00:00:15.072 --> 00:00:21.014
The simplest way is Euler’s method, and we will also measure how accurate it is.

5
00:00:21.264 --> 00:00:26.064
At the starting point the slope is known: it is the value of f there.

6
00:00:26.064 --> 00:00:32.760
We take a short step h along the tangent: the new y equals the old y plus h times f.

7
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.592
Then we repeat from the new point.

8
00:00:35.592 --> 00:00:39.422
Joining the points gives the Euler polygon.

9
00:00:39.672 --> 00:00:46.752
Problem: y prime equals x plus y, y of zero equals one, step zero point one.

10
00:00:46.752 --> 00:00:53.414
First step: f at zero, one equals one, so y one equals one point one.

11
00:00:53.664 --> 00:01:00.072
Second step: the slope is zero point one plus one point one, that is one point two.

12
00:01:00.072 --> 00:01:03.432
So y two equals one point two two.

13
00:01:03.432 --> 00:01:10.814
Continuing for five steps, at x equal to zero point five we get about one point seven two one.

14
00:01:11.064 --> 00:01:20.328
The exact solution is y equals two e to the x minus x minus one; at zero point five it is one point seven nine seven.

15
00:01:20.328 --> 00:01:24.432
The error is about zero point zero seven six.

16
00:01:24.432 --> 00:01:30.470
Halving the step roughly halves the error: this is first order, big O of h.

17
00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:36.504
The graph shows the direction field and the exact integral curve through zero, one.

18
00:01:36.504 --> 00:01:41.856
The Euler polygon follows tangents of a convex curve and stays below it.

19
00:01:41.856 --> 00:01:48.120
The local error per step is proportional to h squared, but there are one over h steps,

20
00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:52.094
so the global error is proportional to h.

21
00:01:52.344 --> 00:01:54.576
Now check yourself.

22
00:01:54.576 --> 00:01:59.280
How does the global error of Euler’s method change when the step is halved?

23
00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.328
Pause the video, choose your answer,

24
00:02:02.328 --> 00:02:08.006
then read the explanation and go back to the relevant part of the lesson if needed.
